{
  "version": 1,
  "title": "IOMMU and VFIO passthrough",
  "hook": "How do the IOMMU and VFIO let a guest own a PCIe device safely?",
  "topic": "virtualization/isolation/iommu-vfio-passthrough",
  "tags": [
    "virtualization",
    "iommu",
    "vfio"
  ],
  "level": "advanced",
  "series": "virtualization-internals",
  "series_index": 5,
  "scenes": [
    {
      "duration_ms": 12000,
      "caption": "A DMA-capable device can read any physical memory unless an IOMMU intervenes.",
      "narration": "Handing a device to a guest is dangerous, because devices do DMA with physical addresses. The IOMMU sits between device and RAM and translates device addresses through per-device page tables.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "DMA needs a gatekeeper",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "dev",
          "type": "box",
          "text": "PCIe device (DMA)",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 20.0,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 11,
          "color": "orange",
          "at": 600,
          "enter": "pop"
        },
        {
          "id": "mmu",
          "type": "box",
          "text": "IOMMU: VT-d / AMD-Vi",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 40.0,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 11,
          "color": "blue",
          "at": 1900,
          "enter": "pop"
        },
        {
          "id": "ram",
          "type": "box",
          "text": "Host RAM",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 60.0,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 11,
          "color": "green",
          "at": 3200,
          "enter": "pop"
        },
        {
          "id": "dev-mmu",
          "type": "arrow",
          "from": "dev",
          "to": "mmu",
          "at": 2500,
          "enter": "draw",
          "label": "IOVA"
        },
        {
          "id": "mmu-ram",
          "type": "arrow",
          "from": "mmu",
          "to": "ram",
          "at": 3800,
          "enter": "draw",
          "label": "PA"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 12000,
      "caption": "The unit of isolation is the IOMMU group: devices that cannot be told apart.",
      "narration": "VFIO hands out whole IOMMU groups, the smallest set of devices the hardware can isolate from each other. Missing ACS on a bridge can put several devices in one group.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "IOMMU groups",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "g",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "text": "/sys/kernel/iommu_groups/14/devices/\n  0000:01:00.0  (GPU)\n  0000:01:00.1  (audio)",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 20.0,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 600,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "n",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Bind each to vfio-pci first.",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 43.7,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 1900,
          "enter": "fade",
          "color": "gray"
        },
        {
          "id": "hl-g",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "g",
          "at": 3600
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 12000,
      "caption": "A VMM opens a container and group, selects Type1, then maps DMA ranges.",
      "narration": "The VMM opens the VFIO container and the group, joins them, and selects the Type1 IOMMU. It then gets the device fd, and calls MAP_DMA to install IOVA mappings.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "VFIO ioctl flow",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "v",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "text": "ioctl(group, VFIO_GROUP_SET_CONTAINER, &c);\nioctl(c, VFIO_SET_IOMMU, VFIO_TYPE1_IOMMU);\nioctl(c, VFIO_IOMMU_MAP_DMA, &map);\ndev = ioctl(group, VFIO_GROUP_GET_DEVICE_FD, id);",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 20.0,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 600,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "hl-v",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "v",
          "at": 2300
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 12000,
      "caption": "Guest physical addresses become IOVAs; VFIO pins the backing host pages.",
      "narration": "MAP_DMA ties an IOVA, usually the guest physical address, to the VMM's virtual memory. VFIO pins those pages, so the device's DMA lands exactly in guest RAM and nowhere else.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Mapping guest RAM",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "m",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "text": "struct vfio_iommu_type1_dma_map {\n  __u64 vaddr;  // VMM memory\n  __u64 iova;   // guest GPA\n  __u64 size;\n};",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 20.0,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 600,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "hl-m",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "m",
          "at": 2300
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 10000,
      "caption": "BARs map into the guest; MSIs are remapped and routed to the vCPU via eventfd.",
      "narration": "The device's BARs are mmapped into the guest. Interrupts become eventfds that KVM injects as irqfds, and interrupt remapping stops the device from forging interrupts.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "MMIO and interrupts",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "i",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "BAR regions: VFIO_DEVICE_GET_REGION_INFO",
            "IRQs: VFIO_DEVICE_SET_IRQS + eventfd",
            "Interrupt remapping blocks spoofed MSIs"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 20.0,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 600,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "color": "purple"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "takeaway": "The IOMMU confines device DMA to guest memory, and VFIO is the userspace API that programs it safely.",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/driver-api/vfio.html"
  ]
}